  "9b6c7383-da8b-4165-bf1d-dc3acbd90254": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p38-constant-solutions-theory",
     "replacement": "When we divide by $g(y)$ to separate variables, we assume $g(y)\\ne 0$.\n\nEach constant $a$ with $g(a)=0$ gives a **constant solution** $y=a$, because then $y'=f(x)g(a)=0$.\n\nSeparation can lose this solution, so check for it separately. A particular value of the arbitrary constant sometimes recovers it.\n\nFind constant solutions by inspection or by solving $g(y)=0$.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p38-constant-solutions-theory",
     "targetFingerprint": "a8063c01a4ebda30d9c90a3065e5ab6b67c4bb2d7595a2da1f8723356e3bd494",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-4-differential-equations-2-v1/20261111-125045",
   "revision": 8
  },
  "674a1592-f015-4339-8a62-12f05db14e0e": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p60-logistic-definition",
     "nodeId": "p60-logistic-static",
     "replacement": "$$\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP\\left(1-\\frac{P}{C}\\right)$$\n\nThe **logistic equation** models a population $P$ at time $t$ whose growth is limited by a **carrying capacity** $C$, the largest population the environment can support. For logistic growth, $k>0$ and $C>0$.\n\n$kP$ represents exponential growth, and $\\left(1-\\frac{P}{C}\\right)$ represents the limits of the environment.\n\nWhen $P=C$, $\\left(1-\\frac{P}{C}\\right)=0$ and growth stops.\n\nStarting from a small positive population, growth is slow at first because few individuals reproduce, speeds up as the population grows, then slows as $P$ approaches $C$.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p60-logistic-definition",
     "targetFingerprint": "6d6df6b62709128ec6d4fa23b5eca8e4c301d4509e8e248eaf1a60a05162013b",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-4-differential-equations-2-v1/20261111-125045",
   "revision": 8
  },
  "c8640a3c-8942-4099-9aba-271bdd72d410": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p60-logistic-conclusion",
     "replacement": "The logistic equation has constant solutions at $P=0$ and $P=C$. These are horizontal isoclines, lines along which the slope is zero. Every solution with a positive initial population tends towards $P=C$ as time increases.\n\nUsually, the scenario limits the domain and range to $t\\geq0$ and $P>0$.\n\n**Alternate notation:**\n\n$P'=kP(C-P)$: the factor $1/C$ is absorbed into the constant, so this $k$ differs from the original.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p60-logistic-conclusion",
     "targetFingerprint": "c4e2caae088f9244efede69bcba78f83241b47859cc36356f2eaac009baf893b",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6ext1-4-differential-equations-2-v1/20261111-125045",
